{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Teaching in Tech-Rich Classrooms With TeachGen@i","title":"TeachGen@i Scenario Setup","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting teachgen@i scenario setup to TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"02","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_scenario_setup","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/02/01"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# TeachGen@i Scenario Setup\n\n![TeachGen@i Scenario Setup textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/02-02-teachgen-i-scenario-setup.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA generative planning tool produces output that is only as grounded as the situation you hand it. \"Scenario setup\" is the act of writing that situation down: the grade, the time block, the prior lesson, the room's constraints, and the students who must be served. It is the single highest-leverage move in AI-assisted planning, because an AI cannot see your room. It fills every gap you leave with a statistical default, usually an affluent, well-resourced, on-grade-level classroom that does not exist. A teacher who skips scenario setup is not collaborating with the tool; they are accepting the average classroom baked into its training data.\n\nThis section treats scenario setup as the foundation on which every later decision rests. The artifact is a prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions, and the transcript begins with the scenario you supplied. A reviewer reading it should be able to tell, from the first prompt alone, that the plan that follows was built for a specific room rather than a generic one.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA first-year teacher opens TeachGen@i and types \"Plan a fractions lesson.\" The result is fluent and confident: a 60-minute block, students working in pairs on laptops, an opening that assumes everyone already understands equivalent fractions. None of it fits her room. Her period is 38 minutes, the laptop cart is checked out that day, a third of her class reads below grade level, and yesterday's lesson only introduced the words numerator and denominator. The plan was not wrong because the tool is bad; it was wrong because she gave it an empty scenario and it filled the emptiness with a default classroom. The fix is not a better tool. It is a better scenario.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nScenario setup means converting the facts of your room into constraints the tool must honor, and distinguishing the facts that bind from the facts that are merely flavor. A complete scenario carries four kinds of information, and a thin prompt is usually missing three of them.\n\n| Scenario element | The binding fact | What the AI defaults to if you omit it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Time and structure | \"38-minute single period, no block\" | A roomy 60-minute lesson with transitions you do not have |\n| Resources present today | \"No student devices; one projector\" | One-to-one laptops and reliable bandwidth |\n| Learner profile (de-identified) | \"About a third read below grade level\" | An on-grade-level, monolingual class |\n| Prior knowledge | \"Yesterday we named numerator and denominator only\" | Mastery of prerequisites the class has not met |\n\nTwo disciplines govern how you write these down. TPACK reminds you that the content prerequisite (what students already know) is a scenario fact, not a nicety; an AI that assumes mastery will plan the wrong lesson. Critical edtech studies and datafication remind you to de-identify: you encode \"about a third read below grade level\" rather than naming students, because the transcript may be stored by a vendor and a real classroom profile should never leave the room attached to names. A scenario is complete when omitting any one element would let the tool substitute a default that does not match your room.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher writes: 'Plan an engaging, rigorous, differentiated 45-minute reading lesson.' Which part of this is the only true binding fact the tool can act on?\" explanation=\"Engaging, rigorous, and differentiated are adjectives, not constraints; the tool cannot convert them into a number of minutes or a learner profile. Only '45-minute' is a binding fact. The rest must be encoded as resources, learner profile, and prerequisites or the tool will fill them with defaults.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The 45-minute time block\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The word rigorous, because it sets a difficulty target\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The word differentiated, because it specifies the learner profile\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one annotated judgment call on setting up a scenario. A fourth-grade teacher wants TeachGen@i to draft a small-group fractions lesson and records the prompt transcript as evidence.\n\n**Step 1. Write the before scenario.** The first prompt reads: \"Plan a fractions lesson.\" Annotated in the margin: too thin. No grade, no prior lesson, no time, no constraint. The plan that came back assumed a 60-minute block, one-to-one laptops, and students already comfortable with equivalent fractions.\n\n**Step 2. Name what the scenario must carry.** The teacher lists the binding facts: grade 4, 38-minute period, no devices that day, about a third of the class reading below grade level, yesterday's lesson introduced numerators and denominators only.\n\n**Step 3. Make the before/after edit on the transcript.**\n\n> **Before:** \"Plan a fractions lesson.\"\n\n> **After:** \"Plan a 38-minute grade-4 lesson on comparing fractions with the same denominator. No student devices today. About one third of the class reads below grade level, so keep written directions short and add a visual model. Yesterday we only named numerators and denominators; do not assume equivalence.\"\n\n**Step 4. Record one rejected suggestion.** TeachGen@i proposed a paired laptop sorting game. The teacher rejected it on the transcript: \"Cut, violates the no-device constraint and would exclude the whole class today.\" The de-identified phrasing (\"about one third read below grade level\") protects students while still steering the plan. A mentor reading the transcript can see the scenario, the added constraints, and the reasoned rejection.\n\nMatch each scenario fact the teacher supplied to the specific default it blocks. Naming the blocked default is what proves the fact was load-bearing.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"38-minute single period\",\"definition\":\"Blocks the default roomy 60-minute lesson with transitions you do not have\"},{\"term\":\"No student devices today\",\"definition\":\"Blocks the default assumption of one-to-one laptops and reliable bandwidth\"},{\"term\":\"About one third read below grade level\",\"definition\":\"Blocks the default on-grade-level, monolingual class\"},{\"term\":\"Yesterday we only named numerator and denominator\",\"definition\":\"Blocks the default assumption that students have mastered equivalence\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nState the scenario before you state the request, and write every binding fact as a hard limit the tool may not relax. The order matters: a request placed before its constraints invites the tool to optimize for an ideal room and then bolt your room on afterward. Put the room first.\n\nWhen the transcript looks finished, audit the opening scenario against four questions:\n\n1. **Time:** Did I give the real minutes, not a generous estimate?\n2. **Resources:** Did I name what is missing today (devices, bandwidth), not just what exists in theory?\n3. **Learners:** Did I encode the profile in de-identified, actionable terms the tool can plan around?\n4. **Prerequisites:** Did I tell the tool what students do NOT yet know, so it cannot assume mastery?\n\nIf any answer is no, the plan that follows is built for a room other than yours.\n\nStep through the choices a teacher faces when the first plan does not fit, and watch how each move either grounds the scenario or hands more decisions to the tool's defaults.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Grounding a thin scenario\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"You typed \\\"Plan a fractions lesson\\\" and got a confident 60-minute, one-to-one-laptop plan that assumes mastery of equivalent fractions. Your period is 38 minutes, the laptop cart is gone today, and yesterday you only named numerator and denominator. What do you do first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep the plan and just trim activities to fit 38 minutes\",\"next\":\"trim\",\"feedback\":\"Trimming treats the symptom. The plan is still built for a different room: it assumes devices you do not have and prerequisites the class has not met.\"},{\"label\":\"Rewrite the prompt to state the room first: time, missing resources, de-identified profile, prior knowledge\",\"next\":\"reprompt\",\"feedback\":\"You put the room before the request, so the tool can no longer substitute defaults for the facts you supplied.\"},{\"label\":\"Add the students’ names and reading scores so the tool can personalize\",\"next\":\"names\",\"feedback\":\"This over-shares. A vendor may store the transcript, so a real profile attached to names should never leave the room.\"}]},\"trim\":{\"prompt\":\"The trimmed plan still opens by assuming students know equivalence, and the first activity needs laptops. Students stall. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Rewrite the prompt with the four scenario elements\",\"next\":\"reprompt\",\"feedback\":\"This is the real fix: state the binding facts as hard limits the tool may not relax.\"}]},\"names\":{\"prompt\":\"You catch yourself before sending. How do you encode the profile safely?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Use de-identified counts and needs: ’about one third read below grade level’\",\"next\":\"reprompt\",\"feedback\":\"De-identified phrasing still steers the plan while protecting students if the transcript is stored.\"}]},\"reprompt\":{\"prompt\":\"Your grounded prompt is ready. The tool now proposes a paired laptop sorting game. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reject it on the transcript and name the binding fact: no devices today\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"You blocked a default and left the reason visible, so a mentor can audit the judgment.\"},{\"label\":\"Accept it; a game will boost engagement\",\"next\":\"trim\",\"feedback\":\"It violates your no-device fact and would exclude the whole class today. Engagement is not a binding fact.\"}]},\"done\":{\"prompt\":\"The transcript now shows the thin first prompt, the grounded rewrite, the de-identified profile, and one blocked default with its reason.\",\"feedback\":\"This is scenario setup as a method: state the room before the request, encode what is missing, block the defaults, and leave the repair visible.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nThe first misreading is to mistake adjectives for constraints. \"Plan an engaging, rigorous, differentiated lesson\" tells the tool nothing it can act on; engaging is not a number of minutes and differentiated is not a learner profile. The second is to treat a fluent output as a sign the scenario was adequate. Fluency only proves the model wrote confidently; it says nothing about whether the plan fits your 38 minutes or your missing laptops. A polished plan built on a thin scenario is the most dangerous case, because its confidence hides the gap it filled with defaults.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake a thin prompt apart and rebuild it. Start with \"Make a reading lesson for my class.\" A reviewer cannot evaluate this because the scenario is invisible. Rebuild it element by element. Time: \"in a 45-minute period.\" Resources: \"I have a class set of the novel but no devices.\" Learners: \"five multilingual learners new to academic English, and two students with IEPs allowing extended response time.\" Prerequisites: \"students have read chapters one through three but have not analyzed theme before.\" The rebuilt prompt is longer, but every clause is a constraint the tool must satisfy and a reviewer can check. The transcript should show this rebuild, not just the final clean prompt, because the rebuild is the evidence of judgment.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: in the left column list each scenario fact you supplied; in the right column write the default the AI would have used if you had left that fact out. For the fractions case, the left column says \"no devices today\" and the right column says \"the tool would have assumed one-to-one laptops.\" A strong reader can look at any AI plan and reverse-engineer the missing scenario by spotting the defaults: a 60-minute structure betrays an unstated time, a laptop activity betrays an unstated resource assumption. Your transcript is strong when a reader can see exactly which defaults you blocked.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong scenario transcript shows the thin first prompt, not just the polished final one, so the reviewer can see what you fixed. It encodes the learner profile in de-identified terms (counts and needs, never names), proving you protected students while still informing the plan. And it names at least one default the tool reached for and you blocked (the assumed devices, the assumed prerequisite mastery), with the binding fact that ruled it out. Polish is the clean final prompt; evidence is the visible repair from thin to grounded.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same setup task from a fourth-grade math lesson to a high-school chemistry lab. The four scenario elements still apply, but their content shifts and a fifth pressure appears: safety constraints (no open flame near the new student who has not completed lab-safety training) become a binding fact the tool will never infer on its own. The skill transfers (state the room before the request, encode what is missing, block the defaults), but the specific facts do not. If you can list the chemistry scenario's binding facts without copying the math example's, you have learned scenario setup as a method rather than as a template.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A peer hands the AI a thin prompt, gets a fluent, polished lesson, and concludes the scenario must have been adequate. In two or three sentences, explain why a fluent output is not evidence that the scenario was complete, and name one default you would reverse-engineer from the plan to expose the missing fact.\" answer=\"Fluency only proves the model wrote confidently; it says nothing about whether the plan fits the real room. A polished plan built on a thin scenario is the most dangerous case because its confidence hides the defaults it inserted. I would read the plan for tells: a 60-minute structure betrays an unstated time, and a laptop activity betrays an assumed one-to-one device resource the room may not have.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/02-02-teachgen-i-scenario-setup-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"02.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions for CAT 531 02.02. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_scenario_setup\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your transcript opens with all four scenario elements (time, resources today, de-identified learner profile, prior knowledge), shows the thin-to-grounded repair, and blocks at least one tool default with the binding fact that ruled it out.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_scenario_setup\", \"cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_scenario_setup","stem":"A teacher opens TeachGen@i and types: \"Plan a fractions lesson.\" The transcript shows a polished plan came back. Why is this scenario setup weak?","options":["Fractions are too easy for a generative tool to plan.","It names a topic but no grade band, prior lesson, time block, or constraint, so the plan cannot fit a real class.","The teacher should have asked for two lessons at once.","TeachGen@i should never be used for math."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A scenario the AI can usefully plan for specifies the learners and setting. A bare topic produces a generic plan no matter how polished it looks.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_generic_scenario","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_scenario_setup","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_scenario_setup","stem":"Which scenario detail is safe and useful to include when setting up a TeachGen@i prompt for a planning task?","options":["\"Marcus R. has an IEP for dyslexia and scored 62% on the unit test.\"","\"About a third of the class reads two grade levels below the text, and we have no one-to-one devices.\"","A screenshot of the class roster with names and student IDs.","The teacher's own login credentials so the tool can pull records."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Describe learner needs in de-identified, aggregate terms. Naming individual students or pasting records into a generative tool is a privacy violation, not a setup detail.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_invented_student_data","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_scenario_setup","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"A teacher generates a plan, then realizes the period is only 40 minutes and devices are banned that day, but the plan assumes 60 minutes of laptop work. What does this reveal about the scenario setup?","options":["TeachGen@i is unreliable and should not be used for planning.","The binding constraints were left out of the scenario, so the fluent plan is not usable; constraints belong in the setup first.","The teacher should accept the plan and find extra time.","The plan is fine because it was internally consistent."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Hard constraints (time block, device policy, target standard) belong in the scenario before generation. A plan built on missing constraints reads well but fails in the room.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_constraints_as_afterthought","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_scenario_setup","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"In a TeachGen@i prompt transcript submitted as evidence, which entry best documents a defensible scenario-setup decision?","options":["\"Prompt: plan a lesson. Output: looked good. Submitted.\"","The original scenario, the line where the teacher added the 40-minute and no-device constraints, and a note explaining a generated grouping suggestion was rejected because it assumed reading levels the class does not have.","A clean final plan with no prompt history.","A note that the teacher tried three tools and picked the fastest."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible transcript shows the scenario, the constraints the teacher added, and at least one rejected suggestion with the reason, so a mentor can audit the judgment.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_2_teachgen_i_scenario_setup_constraints_as_afterthought","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_scenario_setup","evidence_based_revision"]}]}}